INT. CITY PAROLE OFFICE - RECEPTION - MORNING

A beige room full of bad lighting and worse chairs. Two teenagers in the corner share earbuds and a bag of chips. A janitor's mop bucket sits unattended by the water cooler.

Frankie Dunn, 30s, charming and doomed, bursts through the door with a coffee in each hand and a paper bag in his teeth. He trips over the mop bucket. Water SLOSHES across the floor.

FRANKIE
(through the bag)
Mmf! Mmmf!

He spits the bag out. Behind the desk, Officer Priya Nair, 30s, unflappable and unimpressed, looks up from a stack of files.

PRIYA
Mr. Dunn. You're eleven minutes late.

FRANKIE
Priya! Officer Priya! Light of my life! I brought you a latte, the good kind, with the little heart in the foam.

PRIYA
It's a cortado. And I'm not a morning person.

FRANKIE
I know. That's why I got two. One for you, one for me, one for the guy who's gonna ask me why I brought two.

PRIYA
That's three, Frankie.

FRANKIE
(looking at his hands)
Is it? Huh. I only have two hands.

He slips on the wet floor, the coffees fly, and both cups SPLAT against the wall. The teenagers gasp. One of them films it.

THE TEENAGERS
(together, delighted)
Whoa.

PRIYA
(pinching the bridge of her nose)
Frankie. Sit down.

FRANKIE
(dripping)
Yes, ma'am. Absolutely, ma'am. Sitting.

He plops into a chair. It COLLAPSES with a CRACK. He lands on the floor, legs in the air.

PRIYA
(not even blinking)
I'm adding that to the report.

FRANKIE
(from the floor)
Priya. I need to tell you something.

PRIYA
You need to give me your check-in form.

FRANKIE
I have it. It's in my pocket. It's a little wet.

He fishes out a soggy paper ball and holds it up like a trophy. She takes it between two fingers.

PRIYA
This is a tax receipt for a hot dog.

FRANKIE
It was a very good hot dog.

PRIYA
(finally, a crack of a smile)
You are the worst parolee I have ever had.

FRANKIE
(sitting up, earnest)
But am I your favorite?

PRIYA
(looking away, fighting it)
That's not a thing.

FRANKIE
That is totally a thing. You're smiling.

PRIYA
I'm not.

FRANKIE
You are! Your whole face is doing it.

The door BANGS open. THE GUARDS, two enormous men in matching uniforms, stand in the frame.

THE GUARDS
(together, deadpan)
Everything okay in here?

PRIYA
(straightening, all business)
Fine. Mr. Dunn was just leaving.

FRANKIE
(scrambling up)
I was? Oh. I was. Yes. Leaving. Great. Love that for me.

PRIYA
(stopping him with one finger)
Thursday. Ten sharp. Bring a real receipt.

FRANKIE
(beaming)
Bring you a coffee too?

PRIYA
(sighing, a warmth she can't hide)
Just the one this time, Frankie.

FRANKIE
(backing out the door, bowing)
One coffee, Priya. One. You got it.

He steps in the mop bucket again. The BUCKET CLANGS. The door SLAMS behind him. The teenagers burst into applause.

PRIYA
(to the room, under her breath)
Unbelievable.

She picks up the soggy receipt, studies it, and tucks it into her drawer instead of the trash.
